    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums1 = [1, 3], nums2 = [2]           ->  2.0\nnums1 = [1, 2], nums2 = [3, 4]        ->  2.5\nnums1 = [1, 3, 8, 9], nums2 = [2, 4, 5, 7, 10]  ->  5.0"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: hard binary search"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The last two problems are the hardest binary searches interviews ask for. Neither searches for a value in an array. This one searches for a **cut position** that splits two arrays correctly; the next searches a range of **distances**, counting with two pointers at each step. Both still come down to the same question: which half of the search space can safely be thrown away?"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: binary search the cut"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The median splits all `m + n` values into a left half and a right half where **every left value is at most every right value**. Suppose the left half takes `i` values from `nums1` and `j` from `nums2`, with `i + j = half` (half the total, rounded up). Because both arrays are sorted, the cut is correct exactly when `nums1[i−1] <= nums2[j]` and `nums2[j−1] <= nums1[i]` — the largest on each left side doesn't pass the smallest on the other right side. If `nums1[i−1]` is too big, `i` is too large; otherwise `i` is too small. That's a binary search on `i` alone. Search the **shorter** array so `j` always stays in range."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Merge, then take the middle"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findMedianSortedArrays(nums1, nums2) {\n  const merged = [];\n  let i = 0, j = 0;\n  while (i < nums1.length || j < nums2.length) {\n    if (j === nums2.length || (i < nums1.length && nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) merged.push(nums1[i++]);\n    else merged.push(nums2[j++]);\n  }\n  const mid = Math.floor(merged.length / 2);\n  return merged.length % 2 ? merged[mid] : (merged[mid - 1] + merged[mid]) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_median_sorted_arrays(nums1, nums2):\n    merged, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(nums1) or j < len(nums2):\n        if j == len(nums2) or (i < len(nums1) and nums1[i] <= nums2[j]):\n            merged.append(nums1[i]); i += 1\n        else:\n            merged.append(nums2[j]); j += 1\n    mid = len(merged) // 2\n    return float(merged[mid]) if len(merged) % 2 else (merged[mid - 1] + merged[mid]) / 2"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {\n    int[] merged = new int[nums1.length + nums2.length];\n    int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;\n    while (i < nums1.length || j < nums2.length) {\n        if (j == nums2.length || (i < nums1.length && nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) merged[k++] = nums1[i++];\n        else merged[k++] = nums2[j++];\n    }\n    int mid = merged.length / 2;\n    return merged.length % 2 == 1 ? merged[mid] : (merged[mid - 1] + (double) merged[mid]) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {\n    vector<int> merged(nums1.size() + nums2.size());\n    merge(nums1.begin(), nums1.end(), nums2.begin(), nums2.end(), merged.begin());\n    int mid = merged.size() / 2;\n    return merged.size() % 2 ? merged[mid] : (merged[mid - 1] + (double)merged[mid]) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(m + n), space O(m + n).**"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Walk to the middle without storing"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Only the one or two middle values matter, so run the merge's two pointers just far enough and remember the last two values passed."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findMedianSortedArrays(nums1, nums2) {\n  const total = nums1.length + nums2.length;\n  let i = 0, j = 0, prev = 0, cur = 0;\n  for (let step = 0; step <= Math.floor(total / 2); step++) {   // walk to the middle\n    prev = cur;\n    if (j === nums2.length || (i < nums1.length && nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) cur = nums1[i++];\n    else cur = nums2[j++];\n  }\n  return total % 2 ? cur : (prev + cur) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_median_sorted_arrays(nums1, nums2):\n    total = len(nums1) + len(nums2)\n    i = j = prev = cur = 0\n    for _ in range(total // 2 + 1):       # walk to the middle\n        prev = cur\n        if j == len(nums2) or (i < len(nums1) and nums1[i] <= nums2[j]):\n            cur = nums1[i]; i += 1\n        else:\n            cur = nums2[j]; j += 1\n    return float(cur) if total % 2 else (prev + cur) / 2"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {\n    int total = nums1.length + nums2.length;\n    int i = 0, j = 0, prev = 0, cur = 0;\n    for (int step = 0; step <= total / 2; step++) {   // walk to the middle\n        prev = cur;\n        if (j == nums2.length || (i < nums1.length && nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) cur = nums1[i++];\n        else cur = nums2[j++];\n    }\n    return total % 2 == 1 ? cur : (prev + (double) cur) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {\n    int total = nums1.size() + nums2.size();\n    int i = 0, j = 0, prev = 0, cur = 0;\n    for (int step = 0; step <= total / 2; step++) {   // walk to the middle\n        prev = cur;\n        if (j == (int)nums2.size() || (i < (int)nums1.size() && nums1[i] <= nums2[j])) cur = nums1[i++];\n        else cur = nums2[j++];\n    }\n    return total % 2 ? cur : (prev + (double)cur) / 2;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(m + n), space O(1).** A good first answer — then the interviewer asks for O(log)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Binary search the cut in the shorter array"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "median-two-arrays",
      "caption": "Try a cut in the shorter array, take the rest from the longer one, and check the four values around the cuts."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findMedianSortedArrays(nums1, nums2) {\n  if (nums1.length > nums2.length) return findMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1);   // search the shorter one\n  const m = nums1.length, n = nums2.length;\n  const half = Math.floor((m + n + 1) / 2);      // how many values go in the left half\n  let low = 0, high = m;\n  while (low <= high) {\n    const i = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);      // take i values from nums1 ...\n    const j = half - i;                          // ... and the rest from nums2\n    const left1 = i > 0 ? nums1[i - 1] : -Infinity;\n    const right1 = i < m ? nums1[i] : Infinity;\n    const left2 = j > 0 ? nums2[j - 1] : -Infinity;\n    const right2 = j < n ? nums2[j] : Infinity;\n    if (left1 <= right2 && left2 <= right1) {    // every left value <= every right value\n      if ((m + n) % 2 === 1) return Math.max(left1, left2);\n      return (Math.max(left1, left2) + Math.min(right1, right2)) / 2;\n    }\n    if (left1 > right2) high = i - 1;            // took too many from nums1\n    else low = i + 1;                            // took too few from nums1\n  }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_median_sorted_arrays(nums1, nums2):\n    if len(nums1) > len(nums2):\n        nums1, nums2 = nums2, nums1       # search the shorter one\n    m, n = len(nums1), len(nums2)\n    half = (m + n + 1) // 2               # how many values go in the left half\n    low, high = 0, m\n    while low <= high:\n        i = (low + high) // 2             # take i values from nums1 ...\n        j = half - i                      # ... and the rest from nums2\n        left1 = nums1[i - 1] if i > 0 else float(\"-inf\")\n        right1 = nums1[i] if i < m else float(\"inf\")\n        left2 = nums2[j - 1] if j > 0 else float(\"-inf\")\n        right2 = nums2[j] if j < n else float(\"inf\")\n        if left1 <= right2 and left2 <= right1:   # every left value <= every right value\n            if (m + n) % 2:\n                return float(max(left1, left2))\n            return (max(left1, left2) + min(right1, right2)) / 2\n        if left1 > right2:\n            high = i - 1                  # took too many from nums1\n        else:\n            low = i + 1                   # took too few from nums1"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {\n    if (nums1.length > nums2.length) return findMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1);   // search the shorter one\n    int m = nums1.length, n = nums2.length;\n    int half = (m + n + 1) / 2;                  // how many values go in the left half\n    int low = 0, high = m;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int i = (low + high) / 2;                // take i values from nums1 ...\n        int j = half - i;                        // ... and the rest from nums2\n        int left1 = i > 0 ? nums1[i - 1] : Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int right1 = i < m ? nums1[i] : Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        int left2 = j > 0 ? nums2[j - 1] : Integer.MIN_VALUE;\n        int right2 = j < n ? nums2[j] : Integer.MAX_VALUE;\n        if (left1 <= right2 && left2 <= right1) {   // every left value <= every right value\n            if ((m + n) % 2 == 1) return Math.max(left1, left2);\n            return (Math.max(left1, left2) + (double) Math.min(right1, right2)) / 2;\n        }\n        if (left1 > right2) high = i - 1;        // took too many from nums1\n        else low = i + 1;                        // took too few from nums1\n    }\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"inputs must be sorted\");\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "double findMedianSortedArrays(vector<int>& nums1, vector<int>& nums2) {\n    if (nums1.size() > nums2.size()) return findMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1);   // search the shorter one\n    int m = nums1.size(), n = nums2.size();\n    int half = (m + n + 1) / 2;                  // how many values go in the left half\n    int low = 0, high = m;\n    while (low <= high) {\n        int i = (low + high) / 2;                // take i values from nums1 ...\n        int j = half - i;                        // ... and the rest from nums2\n        int left1 = i > 0 ? nums1[i - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int right1 = i < m ? nums1[i] : INT_MAX;\n        int left2 = j > 0 ? nums2[j - 1] : INT_MIN;\n        int right2 = j < n ? nums2[j] : INT_MAX;\n        if (left1 <= right2 && left2 <= right1) {   // every left value <= every right value\n            if ((m + n) % 2) return max(left1, left2);\n            return (max(left1, left2) + (double)min(right1, right2)) / 2;\n        }\n        if (left1 > right2) high = i - 1;        // took too many from nums1\n        else low = i + 1;                        // took too few from nums1\n    }\n    return 0;                                    // not reached for sorted inputs\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log min(m, n)), space O(1).** The missing neighbours at the edges count as −∞ and +∞, so a cut at either end of an array still works. The left half gets the extra value when the total is odd, which is why the odd-case median is the largest left value."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Merge, then take the middle",
          "time": "O(m + n)",
          "timeWhy": "Merging reads **every value** of both arrays once.",
          "space": "O(m + n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The **merged array**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Walk to the middle without storing",
          "time": "O(m + n)",
          "timeWhy": "It still steps through about **half of all values**.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Two pointers and the last two values."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Binary search the cut in the shorter array",
          "time": "O(log min(m, n))",
          "timeWhy": "Each guess **halves** the cut positions in the **shorter** array.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only the cut indexes and four neighbours."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**One array empty** — `[]` and `[1, 2, 3]`: the median of the other one.",
        "**All of one array before the other** — `[1, 2]` and `[3, 4]`: the cut lands at an end.",
        "**Different lengths** — the search must run on the shorter array, or `j` goes out of range.",
        "**Duplicates and negatives** — `[1, 1]` and `[1, 1]`; `[-5, 3]` and `[-2]`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
